      "Why tolerate floating-point imprecision at all? Because the alternative — exact representation of every possible fraction — would be impossibly large or slow for general use. Floating point is a deliberate trade: a vast range and fast hardware, in exchange for tiny, well-understood rounding errors. Knowing the trade exists is what separates 'my totals are mysteriously off by a cent' bafflement from 'right, I should be using integer cents here'.",
